1: # P3F.22. The Hopf gluing homomorphism and its Eells--Kuiper normalization
2: 
3: **Proved.** Use the oriented Hopf bundle with Euler class the positive
4: generator of the oriented two-sphere. Orient its total three-sphere by
5: the circle followed by the base, and orient the four-ball which it
6: bounds accordingly. Use the product orientation on each four-ball and
7: the outward-normal-first convention on
8: \(\partial(D^4_u\times D^4_y)\). For the gluing convention
9: \[
10:  \lambda(f)=(D^4_u\times S^3_y)\cup_{\widetilde f}
11:                   (S^3_u\times D^4_y),
12:  \qquad x\in\partial(D^4_u\times S^3_y)
13:        \sim\widetilde f(x),
14: \]
15: where \(\widetilde f\) is a principal-circle lift of
16: \(f\in I(S^2\times S^3)\), the oriented Eells--Kuiper invariant is
17: \[
18:  \mu(\lambda(f))=\frac{A(f)}{28}\pmod{\mathbb Z},
19:  \qquad A(f)=\frac{4k(f)-\ell(f)}{24}.                 \tag{P3F.22.1}
20: \]
21: Consequently \(\lambda\) is a homomorphism to \(\Theta _7\),
22: \(\lambda(a)=0\), and \(\lambda(b)\) is the generator with
23: \(\mu=1/28\). Its kernel is
24: \(\mathbb Za\oplus28\mathbb Zb\).
25: 
26: The assertion is a transfer computation for the specified gluing map.
27: An assignment of its value to a parameter loop also requires that loop's
28: actual oriented return map. In particular, this proposition by itself
29: does not select the sign of the OS47 parameter loop.
30: 
31: ## 1. The marked double and its bordism
32: 
33: Put \(K=S^2\times D^4\), with boundary \(M=S^2\times S^3\), and form
34: \[
35:  N_f=K_+\cup_f(-K_-),\qquad x\in\partial K_+\sim f(x)\in\partial K_-.
36: \]
37: Let \(x\in H^2(N_f;\mathbb Z)\) restrict to the positive generator on
38: both copies of \(S^2\). Mayer--Vietoris and van Kampen give
39: \[
40:  \pi_1(N_f)=0,\qquad H^j(N_f;\mathbb Z)=
41:  \begin{cases}\mathbb Z&j=0,2,4,6,\\0&\text{otherwise}.
42:  \end{cases}
43: \]
44: The two spin structures on the pieces agree on the simply connected
45: boundary, so \(N_f\) is spin. There is a unique
46: \(y\in H^4(N_f;\mathbb Z)\) with \(\langle xy,[N_f]\rangle=1\).
47: 
48: For clarity the required bordism is an actual collar construction,
49: the one in Jin26v2 Lemma6.2. Write an inward collar of \(K\) as
50: \(M\times[0,1]_s\), and identify the two collars at the ends of
51: \(K\times[0,1]_t\) by
52: \((z,s,0)\sim(f(z),s,1)\). Rounding corners gives a seven-manifold.
53: Its \(s=0\) boundary is the mapping torus in the convention
54: \((z,0)\sim(f(z),1)\); its other boundary is \(-N_f\).
55: Indeed, near that collar the orientation is
56: \(\operatorname{or}(M)\,ds\,dt\); contraction with the outward
57: normal \(-\partial_s\) gives
58: \(\operatorname{or}(M)\,dt\). At \(t=0,1\) the other boundary pieces
59: are \(-K\) and \(+K\), respectively, with exactly the displayed
60: identification. The class \(x\) extends, as does the preferred spin
61: structure (choose the appropriate one of the two spin structures of
62: the bordism). This verifies the direction and the orientation of the
63: comparison, not only equality of the unoriented manifolds.
64: 
65: It follows that
66: \[
67:  x^2=k(f)y,\qquad p_1(TN_f)=\ell(f)y.                 \tag{P3F.22.2}
68: \]
69: 
70: ## 2. Removal of the cubic coordinate by an extending map
71: 
72: Set \(f_0=f\circ a^{-k(f)}\). Jin's additive integral coordinates give
73: \[
74:  k(f_0)=0,\qquad \ell(f_0)=\ell(f)-4k(f)=-24A(f).
75: \]
76: The explicit quaternionic map in P3F.4 extends every lift of
77: \(a^n\) over \(D^4_u\times S^3_y\). Apply that extension on the
78: first gluing piece and the identity on the second. With the convention
79: above it identifies \(\lambda(f_0)\) and \(\lambda(f)\) by an
80: orientation-preserving diffeomorphism. This step does not assume in
81: advance that \(\lambda\) is a group homomorphism.
82: 
83: We can therefore suppose \(k=0\). Write \(p=\ell(f_0)\), so that
84: \(x^2=0\) and \(p_1(TN_{f_0})=py\).
85: 
86: ## 3. The surgery and its bounding eight-manifold
87: 
88: Let \(L\to N_{f_0}\) have first Chern class \(x\), let
89: \(E=S(L)\), and let \(D(L)\) be its oriented disc bundle. The
90: preimage of the first \(K=S^2\times D^4\) is
91: \(S^3\times D^4\). Perform the framed surgery which replaces that
92: piece by \(D^4\times S^3\). Its framing is the product framing of
93: the \(D^4\) factor, so the attaching map is completely specified.
94: The result is a homotopy sphere, either by its explicit identification
95: below or by the elementary Mayer--Vietoris computation in P3F.4.
96: 
97: The orientation is relevant. The oriented circle bundle decomposes as
98: \[
99:  E=(S^3\times D^4)_+\cup_{\widetilde f_0}
100:                                       -(S^3\times D^4)_-.
101: \]
102: The boundary of \(S^3\times D^4\) has orientation
103: \(-\operatorname{or}(S^3\times S^3)\), whereas the boundary of
104: \(D^4\times S^3\) has the positive product orientation. Thus the
105: replacement has orientation \(-(D^4\times S^3)\). The oriented
106: surgery sphere is exactly \(-\lambda(f_0)\).
107: 
108: An eight-manifold bounding that surgery sphere is
109: \[
110:  W=D(L)\cup (D^4\times D^4),
111: \]
112: where the four-handle attaches along the specified
113: \(S^3\times D^4\). Equivalently it is obtained by replacing the
114: Hopf-disc neighbourhood of \(S^2\) by
115: \(\mathbb CP^2\times D^4\). This is the explicit construction in
116: Jiang14, proof of Lemma3.5, Case2. It has an embedded
117: \(\mathbb CP^2\) with trivial normal four-plane and a second
118: four-cycle coming from the generator of \(H_4(N_{f_0})\).
119: Their intersection matrix is
120: \[
121:  \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.              \tag{P3F.22.3}
122: \]
123: The first self-intersection is zero by the trivial normal bundle; the
124: second is zero because the disc-bundle Thom map
125: \(H^2(N_{f_0})\to H^4(N_{f_0})\) is multiplication by \(x\), and
126: \(x^2=0\). The mixed intersection is one because the core
127: \(S^2\) is dual to \(y\). These facts also give a direct check of
128: the intersection matrix in the cited proof.
129: 
130: Let \(z\in H^2(W;\mathbb Z)\) be the class extending \(x\), positive
131: on the projective line in that \(\mathbb CP^2\). It lifts
132: \(w_2(TW)\): on \(D(L)\) the latter is \(x\bmod2\), and on
133: \(\mathbb CP^2\times D^4\) it is the hyperplane class modulo two.
134: Let \(u,v\) be the relative degree-four classes dual to the two
135: cycles in (P3F.22.3). The restrictions to those two cycles give
136: \[
137:  z^2=jv,\qquad p_1(TW)=p\,ju+3jv,
138: \]
139: where \(j:H^4(W,\partial W)\to H^4(W)\) is the natural map.
140: Indeed the restrictions of \(z^2\) are \((1,0)\), and those of
141: \(p_1\) are \((3,p)\). Hence
142: \[
143:  \sigma(W)=0,\quad
144:  \langle p_1^2,[W,\partial W]\rangle=6p,\quad
145:  \langle z^2p_1,[W,\partial W]\rangle=p,\quad
146:  \langle z^4,[W,\partial W]\rangle=0.              \tag{P3F.22.4}
147: \]
148: 
149: ## 4. The invariant and the resulting homomorphism
150: 
151: For a spin boundary homotopy seven-sphere and this characteristic
152: extension \(z\), the spin-c Eells--Kuiper formula is
153: \[
154:  \mu(\partial W)=
155:  \frac{p_1^2-4\sigma(W)}{896}
156:  -\frac{z^2p_1}{192}+\frac{z^4}{384}\pmod{\mathbb Z}.
157: \]
158: The formula is Jiang14 Section3.1(v), with its stated hypotheses;
159: here \(\partial W\) is a homotopy sphere, so the required rational
160: relative-to-absolute degree-four map is an isomorphism. Its
161: normalization also follows by subtracting the spin-c index
162: \(\int e^{z/2}\widehat A\) from the signature polynomial.
163: Substitution of (P3F.22.4) gives
164: \[
165:  \mu(-\lambda(f_0))=
166:  \frac{6p}{896}-\frac{p}{192}=\frac{p}{672}.
167: \]
168: Since \(p=-24A(f)\), reversal of the boundary orientation proves
169: (P3F.22.1).
170: 
171: The Eells--Kuiper invariant is additive under connected sum and
172: identifies \(\Theta _7\) with \(\frac1{28}\mathbb Z/\mathbb Z\).
173: For the positive normalization one can also check the Milnor sphere
174: \(S_{2,-1}(\mathbb H)\): its disc bundle has Euler number one,
175: Pontryagin number six and signature one, giving
176: \((36-4)/896=1/28\). Davis82 Section3 computes the same value.
177: Thus (P3F.22.1), together with Jin's additivity of \(A\), proves
178: that \(\lambda\) is a homomorphism and gives its exact image and
179: kernel. No classification statement about circle actions is needed
180: for this last deduction. **End of proof.**
